<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/trigonometry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Trigonometry starts with a right triangle — the ratios of its sides get names: sin =
    opposite/hypotenuse, cos = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan = opposite/adjacent. The revelation is
    that these ratios depend <strong>only on the angle</strong>, not the triangle's size. Wrap
    the triangle around a circle and the ratios become <strong>waves</strong> — the bridge
    between geometry and everything periodic: sound, seasons, alternating current.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two waves, 90° apart in phase — cos is just sin walked a quarter-turn around the circle" widget="plotter" config={trigCfg} />

  <h2>The identities worth internalising</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pythagoras' identity:</strong> sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 — literally the unit circle's equation in disguise.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase shift:</strong> cos θ = sin(θ + 90°). The two curves above are the same wave, offset a quarter cycle.</li>
    <li><strong>Period 360° (2π rad):</strong> the wave repeats; that's why θ is better measured in radians — the natural unit where arc = angle × radius.</li>
    <li><strong>Addition formulas</strong> (sin(a+b) etc.) — the algebra behind every wave-interference result in physics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Solving triangles</h2>
  <p>
    The practical toolkit: <strong>SOH-CAH-TOA</strong> for right triangles, the
    <strong>sine rule</strong> a/sin A = b/sin B for any triangle, and the <strong>cosine
    rule</strong> c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos C — which is Pythagoras with a correction term for
    non-right angles. Surveyors, navigators and astronomers used exactly these three before
    satellites existed.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Algebra and Trigonometry; standard treatments (Maor, Trigonometric Delights).</p>
</TopicPage>
